25
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Scout Master's ship in seven minutes or less.

26
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Reflection is an important aspect of scouting, Discussing with scouts the different things that they're going through and the values that they apply to them.

27
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:46,000
We have a tradition in our troop that we call Vespers. It comes from the Latin word that means evening Vesperis,

28
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:57,000
And that is the root of the English word West, where the sun sets. and gathering for a moment of reflection or a religious service in the evening has a long history and practice and symbolism.

29
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:04,000
You know, the Jewish Sabbath begins at sundown on Friday and Christians of many denominations observe evening Vespers services.

30
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Muslims pray five times a day, the fourth being the sunset prayer. Hindus practice puja or prayer facing west in the evening

31
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:20,000
On the medicine wheel of Native Americans. west is recognized as the direction of change and reflection.

32
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:31,000
The evening is often the best time for scouts to assemble while camping and reflect on that part of the scout oath that pledges duty to God and that part of the scout law that encourages us towards reverence.

33
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:44,000
We usually do this on a Saturday evening after dinner. It lasts for about 15 or 20 minutes. That seems to be the amount of time that makes it meaningful, yet doesn't make it too long,

34
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Depending on the time and the season in the setting. we may gather around a campfire, outside or on a hillside as the sun sets in the summer,

35
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:59,000
In a grove of trees, you know someplace nice. In the wintertime we may be in the warmth of a cabin.

36
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:08,000
We're either sitting or standing in a circle, And I think that's important just because it puts no one in the background and encourages everyone to participate.

37
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Everybody is in the front line. When we have these Vespers observances. I want three things to be present. I want the whole thing to be reasonably predictable.

38
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Scouts like familiarity with something like this. We have a formal opening, We have a predictable progression of the way the thing is going to unfold

39
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:31,000
And we close formally And this encourages scouts that it's not going to last for hours and hours first of all,

40
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:38,000
And it also kind of signals them, once they become familiar with it, that they're going to be expected to participate.

41
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:47,000
I like it to be brief. I've mentioned this a couple of times already. but effective reflections, they need to be focused and relevant.

42
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:52,000
15 or 20 minutes of concentration is within the grasp of most scouts.

43
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,000
20 minutes may be getting a little long. 15 minutes seems to be about right. Sometimes they're 10 minutes.

44
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:05,000
The idea is to honor both their attention span and the desire to impart something to them.

45
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:14,000
And finally, I believe that we need to have some solemnity. It's not necessarily dour and horribly serious-

46
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:22,000
I mean, we'll laugh sometimes during this, It's no big deal- But we need to maintain a respectful, reverent atmosphere

47
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,000
And that's a signal to the scouts that something important is happening.

48
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:32,000
And I can tell you how we do this And you can adapt this however you like. I mean, you may find this useful and you may not,

49
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:41,000
But our scouts keep patrol logs And the patrol log is kind of a combination of a diary and just a record of what they've been up to and what they're doing.

50
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:51,000
Prior to us getting together as a troop, the patrols go off to their campsite and they'll spend 10 or 15 minutes putting their thoughts for the day into their patrol log

51
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:59,000
And them getting together as a patrol and discussing and reflecting on what they did helps prepare them to be a part of this Vespers observance.

52
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Then, once we assemble, the Vespers leader opens the session with a simple statement.

53
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:16,000
We try and repeat this statement almost verbatim because it really helps everybody understand. okay, now we're going to do Vespers and this is what it means,

54
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:22,000
And we'll observe the different traditions of this time that we spend together,

55
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:33,000
And so we say something like this whenever we're camping. we set aside some time to remind us of that part of the scout oath in which we pledge our duty to God and to help other people at all times,

56
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:37,000
And that part of the scout law that encourages us to be reverent.

57
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:44,000
So for the next few minutes, we'll all have the opportunity to reflect and share on why we are scouts and what it means to us.

58
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:51,000
Then, whoever is leading the Vespers- and it can be a youth, it can be an adult, I've led plenty of them,

59
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:54,000
My senior patrol leader has led some of them-

60
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:04,000
But we share a brief thought or reflection on something we've observed or discovered or thought that day to help focus the group.

61
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:10,000
For example, today on our hike, I watched as we followed the trailblazes along the way.

62
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:20,000
These blazes help us follow the trail and they tell us when we're going to have to turn or indicate other trails that are attached to the one that we're on,

63
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:25,000
And I couldn't help but think that there were other things in life that act like trailblazes.

64
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:30,000
They might not be painted on trees, but the scout law is kind of like a set of trailblazes that guide us.

65
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:33,000
What other things in our life, guide us like the scout law does.

66
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:38,000
Can any of you tell me this kind of trailblaze that you follow in life?

67
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:48,000
So that's brief, to the point, focused, and then it opens up the possibility of everybody joining in and participating in a general discussion.

68
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:54,000
Now this can be a bit of a challenge because sometimes scouts will be very quiet and sometimes everybody will want to speak at once.

69
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:04,000
We need to encourage scouts to share their thoughts and ideas and we need to focus these reflections and guide any discussion that arises, because discussions get digressive.

70
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:14,000
We may start out with one idea and somebody may take it down another trail and somebody may take it down another trail and pretty soon we started talking about A and now we're talking about X.

71
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:21,000
Part of the leader's job is to focus everybody back on the central thought.

72
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:29,000
You know that's a worthy subject that you mentioned there, but right now we're talking about this and I'd really like to hear your thoughts on that-

73
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:34,000
And it's important to treat every contribution that a scout makes as sincere,

74
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:40,000
And there's always a. you know every troop has a joker, every troop has a smart algae guy,

75
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:53,000
You know, and if you treat whatever they say is sincere and you ask them questions about it. they get the message pretty quickly that hey, this is not a place where we're going to be joking around about stuff.

76
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:55,000
This is a serious thing.

77
00:09:55,000 --> 00:10:01,000
So you know, they may come up with a little quip or try to impress everybody with their wit,

78
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:05,000
And I would say something now: do you really feel that way

79
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
Or are you just kidding us?

80
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Your cheerfulness is always welcome, but I'm more interested in what you really think, or what you really feel about what we're speaking about right now.

81
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:19,000
Bringing things around kindly is important.

82
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:28,000
Now, once we've heard from several scouts and the discussion and the sharing has started to wane a little bit, we should ask if anyone else has a thought,

83
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:32,000
And if no one else does, we move towards closing.

84
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
Closing can be lots of different things.

85
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:39,000
It may be the scout master's benediction, you know, may the great master of all scouts be with us till we meet again.

86
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:40,000
It can be a prayer.

87
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:43,000
We usually close with a moment of silence.

88
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:51,000
Let's close with a moment of silence in which we remember why we are scouts and take the time to concentrate on what we've shared today and what we've heard this evening.

89
00:10:51,000 --> 00:11:00,000
Now, when we begin to talk about observances like these- the one I'm explaining is not necessarily religious in nature.

90
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:07,000
Now, a lot of people may say it being useful to make this a more specifically religious observance.

91
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:11,000
So we need to be informed by a couple of things,

92
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:22,000
And I found a couple of statements from the BSA that help us identify what's appropriate to put in a religious observance. as scout leaders

93
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
One says-

94
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
There's another statement I found that says this-

95
00:11:52,000 --> 00:12:14,000
And finally, the Boy Scouts of America recognizes the religious element in the training of the member, but it is absolutely non sectarian in its attitude towards that religious training.

96
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
So what's important to take away from this?

97
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:28,000
Well, if we're going to have a specifically religious observance, then we need to make sure that that the content of that religious observance follows the policies that I've just read.

98
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:35,000
A lot of times people substitute the term non denominational for non sectarian. but there's a difference.

99
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:48,000
Non denominational means that if everyone in the group shares the same religious tradition, for instance Christianity, yet is members of different denominations of Christianity-

100
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:59,000
So you know, Catholic, Baptist, Methodist- then the content of this observance should be non denominational.

101
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:04,000
You may use specifically denominational things, but it always has to be couched in the idea of:

102
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:17,000
I'd like to share this. this is part of the denominational tradition that I come from and it may appeal to you. it may not, but this will help you understand what I believe and who I am.

103
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:25,000
But it shouldn't be required that scouts have to adhere to specifically denominational things in that instance-

104
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:33,000
And the instance I'm talking about is where everyone in the group shares one religious tradition, such as Christianity, for instance.

105
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:38,000
If the scouts in your group share different religious traditions-

106
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:46,000
In other words, let's say you have some scouts who are Christians, some scouts who are Jewish, some scouts who are Muslim, some scouts who are Buddhist-

107
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:51,000
We've had all of those different traditions represented in our group over time-

108
00:13:51,000 --> 00:14:02,000
Then our religious observance needs to be specifically non sectarian- non sectarian, not non denominational, but non sectarian.

109
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:09,000
In other words, it shouldn't be all specifically Christian, it shouldn't be all specifically Jewish, it shouldn't be all specifically Muslim.

110
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:13,000
It should be focused on the shared elements of those different religious traditions.

111
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:27,000
You may end up sharing a specifically sectarian thought or idea or practice, but in no way would you want to put it in a form that required people from another religious tradition to adhere to it.

112
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:31,000
So there is a difference between non denominational and non sectarian.

113
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:49,000
As responsible scout leaders, we want to make sure that the content of any religious observance that we create or participate in for our scouts is thoughtful of the different religious traditions, reverent, respectful of the different religious traditions represented by our scouts.

114
00:14:49,000 --> 00:15:09,000
Now, earlier, when I talked about the leaders reflection, I used an example of something that I had thought about that day while we were out hiking. and it's always good to have a short file of different ideas that you can apply to this, just in case you don't come up with anything during the day.

115
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:19,000
And it's always good to share the leadership of this Vespers with other people so that they can have something to say and they can lead a Vespers observance as well.

